A||||3||dnixon||Difficulty starting 87 GT
Z||000000||dnixon||09-04-99||09:30 PM||||I'd really appreciate any help on this:<p>Over the last two years my 87 Fiero GT 2.8 V6 with 135000 km, has occasionally refused to start.   Last occasion, I drove it in the evening to a friend's house, T about 65F, dry weather.   3 hours later it cranked over fine, but wouldn't fire.   The next day, similar weather, it started immediately.   I drove it home.   3 hours later, it cranked and cranked but wouldn't fire.   The next morning it started immediately, but ran a bit rough and stalled at every stop.   If it didn't stall, idle sank to about 300 and fluctuated to about 1600, making about six cycles before settling at 1000.<p>This has happened about four times in the last year or so.<p>Has anyone a clue?   Battery's fine and the fuel pump is audibly running.<p>Could my bad habit of feathering the throttle pedal as I start be a contribution?   I know it's verboten, but it's a habit from Healeys and Jags, with SUs.<p>I'd be really grateful if anyone has any suggestions.
Z||000001||SloPonti||09-04-99||09:38 PM||||you are not supposed to use the gas pedal when starting a fuel injection car!  it is possible that the injectors are starting to go bad!  what octane of gas do you run? the use of lower octane gas will ruin injectors at a faster rate then using higher octane gas but 135,000 miles on the injectors is pretty good!  hope this helps!
Z||000002||dnixon||09-04-99||10:04 PM||||To SloPonti<p>Jeez, was that a fast reply.   Thanks.<p>Actually, it's only 135000 km, not miles, but still, I'll take your advice and have the injectors checked.   I run low octane, as it ran really rough when I used high octane.   I thought that might be due to additives we have in Canada that I think aren't used in the States.   I'd be glad of opinions of the virtues of high versus low octane gas.   I have been told that the computer system should be able to handle both equally well.<p>Thanks again.
Z||000003||SloPonti||09-05-99||08:53 AM||||i have a good feeling it is your injectors!  i am currently studing to be a mechanic and have taken to fuel systems class.  during the class the instructor tried to enforce that the higher the grade of gas the longer the injectors will last!  i have a 84 fiero 4 banger with 100,000 miles on it and no problems yet-but i run 93 octane in it also! i would have them checked out-plus check out the wiring that runs of of them also!<br>hope this works for you
